==Governance==
The sport is governed in Okaiken by the [[Sanese Lurandian Football Association]] (SLFA; {{nihongo|鎧球連盟|Gaikyū Renmei}}), which oversees the professional X-League, the university and high school competitions, and the national team program. The SLFA was founded in 1978 and is a member of the [[Lurandian Football Union]] (LFU), the international governing body for the sport.

At the international level, the [[Lurandian Football Union]] governs the sport across the [[Lurandia]]n continent and sets the official rules of play. The LFU was founded in 1910 in [[Nanaimo]], [[Kaneda]], and currently includes member associations from Kaneda, [[Kazabune]], the [[United Midlands]], and Okaiken (as an associate member).

==Competitions==
===Professional: X-League===
{{Main|X-League}}
The [[X-League]] is the highest level of professional Lurandian Football in Okaiken. Founded in 1985, the league currently comprises twelve corporate-backed teams organized into two divisions. The season runs from September through January, culminating in the [[Rice Bowl]] national championship. The X-League is the second most-watched professional sports league in Okaiken after [[Okaiken Rugby League One]].

===University: Kanto Collegiate League===
University Lurandian Football is contested through the [[Kanto Collegiate Gaikyū League]] ({{nihongo|関東大学鎧球連盟|Kantō Daigaku Gaikyū Renmei}}), which features teams from major universities across the Kanto and Kansai regions. The university season runs from September through November, with the [[Scholar's Bowl]] championship game held in early December. University Lurandian Football is particularly popular among students and alumni, and serves as the primary development pathway for X-League players.

The most prominent university programs include [[Okami Teikoku University]], [[Keio University]], [[Waseda University]], and [[Toyozawa University]]. The annual Okami–Keio matchup, known as the "Scholar's Bowl qualifier," regularly draws crowds of 25,000 and is considered one of the fiercest rivalries in Sanese collegiate sports.

===High school===
High school Lurandian Football is organized through the [[National High School Gaikyū Federation]] ({{nihongo|全国高等学校鎧球連盟|Zenkoku Kōtō Gakkō Gaikyū Renmei}}). The high school season runs from April through July, culminating in the [[Iron Helmet Tournament]] ({{nihongo|鉄兜大会|Tetsukabuto Taikai}}), a nationwide knockout tournament held annually at the [[Okaiken Budokan]] auxiliary fields in [[Okami]]. The tournament is a major scouting event for X-League and university programs.

===National team===
The [[Okaiken national Lurandian Football team]] ({{nihongo|鎧球代表|Gaikyū Daihyō}}), nicknamed the '''Iron Samurais''', represents Okaiken in international Lurandian Football competitions. The team competes in the [[Lurandian Football Championship]], a continental tournament held every four years among the LFU member nations. Okaiken's best result was a runner-up finish at the 2018 championship, losing to host nation [[Kaneda]] in the final in [[Kanakou]].

==Cultural significance==
Lurandian Football occupies a unique position in Okaiken's sporting culture. While rugby union is the most popular team sport, Lurandian Football's deliberate emphasis on armored confrontation, tactical complexity, and specialized positional play has made it a natural expression of the {{nihongo|[[Forge State]]|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology. The sport's reliance on protective equipment—called {{nihongo|鎧|yoroi|armor}}—evokes the nation's warrior heritage, and the strategic, play-calling nature of the game appeals to the same audience that prizes tactical discipline in combat sports.

The [[Rice Bowl]] has become a major cultural event. Held annually on the second Saturday of January, the game virtually stops the nation. Rice Bowl parties are a staple of Sanese social life, and the week preceding the game is marked by extensive media coverage, corporate hospitality events, and public festivities. The game's timing—shortly after the New Year holiday—positions it as the first major social event of the calendar year, and its viewership routinely exceeds 40 million.

The sport's signature rivalry—the decade-long clash between [[Ryuichi Nakada]] of the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]] and the defense of the [[Okami Direwolves]], historically anchored by [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo "The Wall" Shibata]] and [[Naoto Kongo]]—has transcended sports to become a broader cultural narrative. Nakada's offensive brilliance versus the Direwolves' unyielding defensive front is frequently cited in popular media as a metaphor for the Sanese ideals of strategic attack and immovable defense.

